                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Develop arguments based on one or more texts studied. Reading: Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can develop arguments based on reading texts about personal growth (e.g., "Why is it good to learn from mistakes?").<br>Can identify the main ideas of persuasive texts about learning strategies (e.g., "What is one way to improve your grades?").<br>Can demonstrate phonemic awareness by recognizing and correctly pronouncing minimal pair sounds (e.g., /ð/ in "they" and /d/ in "day") to enhance pronunciation accuracy during reading activities.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing pictures of students facing common academic challenges (e.g., a low test score, forgetting homework, struggling with a challenging assignment). Students quickly brainstorm (Think-Pair-Share) one "mistake" they made in a class and what they "learned" from it. The teacher prompts: "What did you learn from this experience?" and encourages students to use vocabulary like 'study' or 'practice'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly skim a short text for its main idea. The teacher also introduces and models the pronunciation of target minimal pair sounds (/ð/ in "they" vs /d/ in "day," /θ/ in "think" vs /s/ in "sink," /v/ in "van" vs /b/ in "ban") by saying words and pointing to their mouth. Additionally, the teacher reviews the pronunciation of past tense verbs ending in "-ed" with examples from previous lessons like "improved" /d/, "wanted" /ɪd/, and "practiced" /t/, ensuring students hear the distinct sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up topic and phonemic sounds.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'mistake' something good or something we learn from?" (Expected: We learn from it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound do you hear at the beginning of 'they'? What sound for 'day'?" (Expected: Different sounds, /ð/ and /d/.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-14 min</td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 2 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short persuasive blog post titled "Turning Academic Mistakes into Milestones" about common study "habits" that lead to "mistakes" and how to achieve "improvement" and "success." The text integrates "past simple" to share experiences (e.g., "I learned to study harder."), "future tense" for improvements (e.g., "I will practice more next time."), and "should" for advice (e.g., "You should ask for feedback.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments related to personal growth and academic improvement. Develop arguments based on the information presented in the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text silently to answer: "What is the main message of this blog post?" (e.g., learning from "mistakes" leads to "improvement").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read specific statements based on the text and mark them as True or False (e.g., "The author believes 'feedback' is not important for 'progress'.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases describing "mistakes" mentioned in the text with the suggested "improvement" (e.g., "Not completing 'homework' carefully" matches with "You should 'study' more 'carefully' and 'practice' daily.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 3 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension to identify main ideas and arguments in persuasive texts. Use of Past simple (experiences), Future tense ("will" for improvements), and "should" (advice). Vocabulary: "mistakes," "grades," "study," "habits," "homework," "feedback," "progress," "improvement," "success," "practice," "improve," "learn," "better," "successful," "challenging," "carefully," "well." Pronunciation of minimal pairs and past tense "-ed" endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading for Detail):</b> Students re-read the blog post and complete cloze sentences using specific vocabulary words from a word bank (mistakes, grades, improvement, habits, feedback, carefully, practice). E.g., "The author suggests that poor study _______ can lead to low _______. But with good _______, students can see great _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Identifying Arguments):</b> In pairs, students identify two main arguments from the text and find a sentence or phrase that supports each argument. They write down the argument and its supporting evidence. E.g., Argument: "Learning from 'mistakes' is crucial for 'progress'." Supporting evidence: "Every time I failed a quiz, I 'learned' to 'study' 'better'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Reading Practice:</b> The teacher writes pairs of minimal words (e.g., "they/day", "think/sink", "van/ban") on the board. Students individually read short sentences from the blog post that include these words or words with similar sounds, consciously focusing on the correct pronunciation of /ð/, /d/, /θ/, /s/, /v/, /b/ and the past tense "-ed" endings (e.g., "She 'improved' her 'grades' because she 'practiced' 'well'."). Teacher monitors and provides corrective 'feedback'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 4 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different short, anonymous anecdote (2-3 sentences) from a student describing an academic "mistake" (e.g., "I forgot to 'study' for a test" or "My 'homework' was not 'well' done, and I got bad 'feedback'").</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Argument Formulation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ol style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads their assigned anecdote and discusses it, identifying the "mistake" and its consequences.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Based on the initial blog post and their own understanding, groups develop at least two main arguments for *why* it's important to learn from this specific mistake, and suggest two pieces of advice or future actions for the student in the anecdote. They must use expressions like "I learned to..." and "You should..." or "Next time, I will...".</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then present their arguments and advice to another group, receiving and giving "constructive feedback" (Sociolinguistic) on the clarity and strength of their points. They focus on using the future tense to describe "improvement" (e.g., "Next time, I will 'study' 'better' to get 'better' 'grades'").</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly links to "Learning from Mistakes" and "Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid..." by having students analyze a specific mistake from a text and propose solutions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing texts, formulating arguments, giving advice, reflecting on learning, and setting goals for "improvement."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 5 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4, focusing on students' ability to identify main ideas and arguments from the provided texts, and their use of target grammar and vocabulary during their discussions and presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student writes an "exit ticket" responding to two prompts based on the day's activities and readings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to accurately identify main ideas and supporting arguments from the texts studied.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., "mistakes," "grades," "improvement," "feedback," "success").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of past simple, future tense ("will"), and "should" for advice in their written responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in expressing arguments and ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstration of phonemic awareness (especially with minimal pairs) during oral components of the task.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one main idea you identified from today's texts about 'learning from mistakes'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one piece of advice you 'should' use to 'improve' your 'grades' based on what we read today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 6 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about identifying arguments in the texts today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will practicing minimal pair sounds help you understand English 'better' when you read?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading comprehension strategies (identifying main ideas, arguments, details) worked well? Were students able to use the target grammar (past simple, future 'will', 'should') and vocabulary when discussing the texts? Was the text level appropriate for A2.3? How effective was the phonemic awareness practice in helping students recognize and pronounce the sounds during reading activities? What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with identifying main arguments in texts?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will actively 'practice' and use the arguments, advice, and vocabulary 'learned' from today's reading to engage in interactive speaking tasks, further consolidating their understanding and applying their reflections on 'learning from mistakes' to personal goals for 'improvement'.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>